  "body": "**What happens:** the work-material table (`crates/tools/src/cutting.rs`, `WorkMaterial`) covers aluminium, steels, cast iron, brass and plastics only, and 29 of 33 catalog tools are for aluminium. A router user cutting wood, MDF or plywood gets either no feeds-and-speeds suggestion or aluminium-based values.\n\n**Wanted:** work materials for softwood, hardwood, MDF, plywood, and router plastics (acrylic, HDPE). Each gets a cutting-speed window and a chip load by cutter diameter, from published machining sources. The suggestion is clamped to the machine's rpm range: a router spindle's minimum is often 6,000-10,000 rpm and its maximum about 24,000. When the clamp changes the result, the feed is recomputed to keep the chip load, and the clamp is reported.\n\n**Safety floor:** this changes posted F and S, so effort:high. A pinning test covers each material and a clamped case.\n\n**Done means:** choosing one of these materials gives a stated, conservative S and F for a router end mill, V-bit and ball nose, clamped to the machine's limits, pinned by tests.\n\nAsked by the user (mkinney), 2026-11-20, for hobby router owners (MisterMaker's WorkBee #3038, Mark's router #1907): \"Mark will prob want him for his router, too. So, it is not a let's wait until later issue; the sooner the better, within reason ofc\".\n\nPart of #3066 (findings: https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/3066#issuecomment-7050655107). The clean-room boundary of #3066 applies: no vendor wording, labels, parameter names or dialog flows.",
